<section class="section" id="argument">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The argument</span>
      <h2>Performance is political</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="argument">
      <div>
        <p>International institutions are essential for tackling the most urgent challenges
          facing the world, from pandemics to humanitarian crises. Yet we know remarkably
          little about when they succeed, when they fail, and why.</p>
        <p>The conventional answer blames the institutions themselves &mdash; the
          <span class="hl">&ldquo;runaway bureaucracy&rdquo;</span> that drifts from the purposes
          its founders intended. The book argues close to the opposite. The most serious threat
          to performance comes from states pursuing narrow national interests through the very
          institutions they created and gave purpose to.</p>
        <p>What protects an institution, on this account, is <span class="hl--coral">de facto
          policy autonomy</span>: not the formal independence written into a charter, but
          autonomy built in practice, through operational alliances with actors above and below
          the state and through work that is genuinely difficult for governments to monitor.</p>
        <p>The theory is tested against a diverse body of evidence &mdash; the most comprehensive
          dataset assembled on the topic, an original survey of institutional staff, and paired
          case studies in global food security and global health.</p>
      </div>
