<section class="section" id="praise">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Praise</span>
      <h2>Endorsements</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="quotes">
      <figure class="quote">
        <blockquote>A fascinating book that stands much of the conventional wisdom on what
          makes for effective international governance on its head. Ranjit Lall has written a
          deeply original and meticulously researched book that sets the standard for future
          work in this area.</blockquote>
        <figcaption>Dani Rodrik &middot; Harvard University</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="quote">
        <blockquote>Ranjit Lall&rsquo;s original and persuasive argument is that institutional
          performance depends on linkages to transnational networks and deep engagement with
          civil society, which enhance policy autonomy and limit state interference. All serious
          students of international institutions should read this book.</blockquote>
        <figcaption>Robert O. Keohane &middot; Princeton University</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="quote">
        <blockquote>The path to more effective international institutions lies not through
          formal institutional rules but through international organizations developing
          operational advantages that states need but cannot interfere with, coupled with
          pursuing projects through informal partnerships with nonstate actors and
          transgovernmental networks. Ranjit Lall supports his persuasive argument with
          extensive data and case studies explaining the comparative performance of a wide
          range of international organizations.</blockquote>
        <figcaption>Duncan Snidal &middot; University of Oxford</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="quote">
        <blockquote>Why do some international institutions perform well at providing critical
          public goods for the world&rsquo;s people, while others fail? Lall provides a powerful
          explanation using new data from multiple sources. International institutions need a
          minimal level of autonomy from the states that created them to function well, and
          they can attain this by employing the same clever strategies that people and
          governments follow: making alliances and exploiting informational advantages. His
          book provides a clear guide for scholars and policymakers on how to make
          international institutions work for the world.</blockquote>
        <figcaption>Helen Milner &middot; Princeton University</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="reviews">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Reviews</span>
      <h2>In the journals</h2>
      <p>Reviews and review essays across international relations, political science, public
        administration, and international law.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="reviews">
      <li class="review">
        <div class="review__head">
          <span class="review__venue">The Review of International Organizations</span>
          <span class="review__by">Felicity Vabulas, Pepperdine University &middot; 2023, 18(4), 783&ndash;787</span>
          <a class="review__link" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s11558-023-09503-6">Read &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <p class="review__q">&hellip; a welcome reminder that performance of IIs has varied
          greatly: many IIs have also succeeded in performing important tasks that make the
          world a better place.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="review">
        <div class="review__head">
          <span class="review__venue">Perspectives on Politics</span>
          <span class="review__by">Yves Steinebach, University of Oslo &middot; 2024</span>
          <a class="review__link" href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/perspectives-on-politics/article/abs/making-international-institutions-work-the-politics-of-performance-by-ranjit-lall-cambridge-cambridge-university-press-2023-412p-13000-cloth/50D1586275ABE54822DE7C048A6E2A7F">Read &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <p class="review__q">Scholars, students, and general readers alike will highly
          appreciate this theory-guided empirical research. Furthermore, the book lays the
          groundwork for new ways of thinking and understanding institutional performance.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="review">
        <div class="review__head">
          <span class="review__venue">International Affairs</span>
          <span class="review__by">Herman T. Salton &middot; 2024, 100(5), 2247&ndash;2248</span>
          <a class="review__link" href="https://academic.oup.com/ia/article-abstract/100/5/2247/7750295">Read &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <p class="review__q">&hellip; this impressive book embodies an important step towards
          greater appreciation of the promises and the limits of cooperating through
          international institutions.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="review">
        <div class="review__head">
          <span class="review__venue">Political Science Quarterly</span>
          <span class="review__by">Edward D. Mansfield, University of Pennsylvania &middot; 2025, 140(2), 327&ndash;341 &middot; review essay</span>
          <a class="review__link" href="https://academic.oup.com/psq/article-abstract/140/2/327/7623184">Read &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <p class="review__q">Makes &ldquo;a significant contribution to our understanding of the
          performance of IOs&rdquo;, and one Mansfield engages at essay length, re-estimating the
          models on Lall&rsquo;s published data.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="review">
        <div class="review__head">
          <span class="review__venue">International Studies Review</span>
          <span class="review__by">Diego Dewar Viscarra, Blavatnik School of Government, Oxford &middot; 2024, 26(2), viae017</span>
          <a class="review__link" href="https://academic.oup.com/isr/article-abstract/26/2/viae017/7682097">Read &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <p class="review__q">The argument &ldquo;advances International Relations theory by
          challenging realism&rdquo;, with the Jekyll and Hyde problem singled out as among the
          book&rsquo;s most relevant contributions.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="review">
        <div class="review__head">
          <span class="review__venue">Governance</span>
          <span class="review__by">Tatiana Cruz and Tana Johnson, University of Wisconsin&ndash;Madison &middot; 2024, 37(2), 681&ndash;683</span>
          <a class="review__link" href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/gove.12861">Read &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <p class="review__q">&ldquo;This book should be read by practitioners and scholars
          alike&rdquo; &mdash; credited with a tightly woven argument and empirics that are both
          diverse and systematic.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="review">
        <div class="review__head">
          <span class="review__venue">International Organizations Law Review</span>
          <span class="review__by">Jan Klabbers, Whewell Professor of International Law, University of Cambridge &middot; 2026, 23, 101&ndash;128</span>
          <a class="review__link" href="https://brill.com/view/journals/iolr/23/1/article-p101_005.xml">Read &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <p class="review__q">The book &ldquo;will become the benchmark for further work on the
          performance of international organizations&rdquo;.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

